# {{.Name}}

A [Forge](https://github.com/rocketinsidecorp/forge-go) application.

## Run

With Docker (the default):

```bash
docker compose up --build
```

Then open `http://127.0.0.1:8080/` to see the Forge welcome screen, or check
the JSON service endpoint:

```bash
curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/status
```
{{- if .DB}}
{{- if .DB.Server}}

This starts {{.DB.Title}}, applies migrations with a one-off `migrate`
container, then starts the app. The database is published on
`127.0.0.1:{{.DBHostPort}}`, a port derived from the application name so it does not
clash with a locally installed server; change it in both `compose.yaml` and
`.env.development` if needed.
{{- else}}

This applies migrations with a one-off `migrate` container, then starts the
app. The SQLite database lives on the `storage` volume; locally, `forge dev`
uses `storage/development.db`.
{{- end}}
{{- end}}

Without Docker:

```bash
{{- if .DB}}
{{- if .DB.Server}}
docker compose up -d {{.DB.Service}}   # or any {{.DB.Title}} matching .env.development
{{- end}}
forge migrate
{{- end}}
forge dev
```

## Develop

```bash
forge test                               # go test ./... with .env.development loaded
forge build                              # static binary in bin/{{.Name}}
docker build -t {{.Name}} .                  # production image
forge generate view welcome --dry-run     # preview files without writing
forge generate view welcome              # a page in app/views/pages/, auto-escaped
forge generate job cleanup --dry-run      # preview a job stub and its tests
forge generate job cleanup               # create files under app/jobs/
forge generate job send_welcome user_id:uuid --queue # a typed handler for the persistent job queue
forge generate controller pages home --public # handlers, routes, views and tests for read-only pages
forge generate mailer user welcome:name  # a mailer with text and HTML templates, previews and tests
forge destroy controller pages home --public # remove what a generator created, unless you edited it
forge routes                             # list the routes found in app/ and cmd/
{{- if .DB}}
forge generate model post title:string --tenant-scoped # migration, domain, application and persistence layers
forge generate migration create_widgets --dry-run # preview exact migration files
forge generate migration create_widgets  # new db/migrations/<timestamp>_create_widgets.{up,down}.sql
forge migrate                            # apply pending migrations
forge migrate status
forge rollback                           # revert the latest migration
{{- end}}
```

## HTML pages

Server-rendered pages live in `app/views`: `layouts/application.gohtml`
wraps every page that defines a `content` block, partials are named
`_NAME.gohtml` (`shared/_flash.gohtml` shows flash messages), and the
stylesheet is `app/assets/application.css`. Build the renderer
once with `views.New(views.WithI18n(translator))` and render pages with
`RenderRequest`. `bootstrap.Configure` installs the locale negotiation,
method override (`_method` in forms), CSRF protection
(`{{"{{"}}csrfField{{"}}"}}` in every form) and flash messages for all
routes. See the Forge views guide (`docs/VIEWS.md`).

## Translations

User-visible text lives in `config/locales`: `en.json`{{if ne .Locale "en"}} and
`{{.Locale}}.json` (the default locale){{end}}, plus scoped files such as
`posts.en.json` that `forge generate scaffold` adds. Each file is one JSON
object whose only key is the locale; keys are dotted paths, `%{name}`
interpolates and `one`/`other` (CLDR plural forms) choose by `count`:

```json
{"pt-BR": {"posts": {"count": {"one": "%{count} post", "other": "%{count} posts"}}}}
```

Pages use `{{"{{"}}t "posts.index.title"{{"}}"}}`,
`{{"{{"}}t "posts.count" "count" .Total{{"}}"}}`, `{{"{{"}}l .CreatedAt "short"{{"}}"}}` and
`{{"{{"}}number .Price 2{{"}}"}}`; Go code uses `i18n.T(r.Context(), "posts.flash.created")`.
Each request gets the locale of the `locale` cookie, then `Accept-Language`,
then the default (`FORGE_I18N_DEFAULT_LOCALE`, else `{{.Locale}}`).

```bash
cp config/locales/en.json config/locales/fr.json   # then translate it and rename the top-level key to "fr"
forge i18n check                                   # keys each locale lacks, plural forms, unused keys
curl -H 'Accept-Language: {{.Locale}}' http://127.0.0.1:8080/
FORGE_I18N_DEFAULT_LOCALE=en forge dev             # serve English by default
```

Missing translations show as `translation missing: LOCALE.KEY` in
development and test and fall back to the default locale in production. See
the Forge i18n guide (`docs/I18N.md`).

## JavaScript and styles

There is no Node toolchain. Static files are embedded in the binary and
served with content-digested names (`/assets/application-<digest>.css`) and
immutable caching; Turbo and Stimulus are vendored by Forge and loaded
through an import map (no inline scripts: the page policy allows the map by
hash).

| Path | Purpose |
| ---- | ------- |
| `app/assets/` | stylesheet and images (add the file name to the `go:embed` line in `assets.go`) |
| `app/javascript/application.js` | entry point: `import "@hotwired/turbo"` and `import "controllers"` |
| `app/javascript/controllers/` | Stimulus controllers, one `NAME_controller.js` each, registered in `index.js` |

Add a Stimulus controller:

```bash
cp app/javascript/controllers/hello_controller.js app/javascript/controllers/clipboard_controller.js
# then add to app/javascript/controllers/index.js:
#   import ClipboardController from "controllers/clipboard_controller"
#   application.register("clipboard", ClipboardController)
```

and use it in a template with `data-controller="clipboard"`. Files in
`controllers/` are embedded and pinned automatically; rebuild (`forge dev`
does) to serve a change under its new digested URL. Real-time updates:
`forge generate scaffold NAME ... --broadcast` (see `docs/VIEWS.md`).
{{- if and .DB .DB.Identity}}

## Sign in

Create the first organization, tenant and owner account once, after the
migrations (the password is prompted for twice, never passed as an argument):

```bash
forge migrate
forge setup --tenant acme --email you@example.com --organization "Acme Inc"
forge dev
```

Then open `http://localhost:8080/session/new` and sign in with organization
`acme`. The owner holds every permission: `setup` grants all registered
permissions to the tenant's `owner` role, and the migrations of
`forge generate resource` and `forge generate scaffold` grant their new
permissions to it. `setup` refuses once a tenant exists. In a container, run
`docker compose run --rm app setup --tenant acme --email you@example.com`, or
pipe the password with `--password-stdin` (or set `FORGE_SETUP_PASSWORD` for
that one command) where there is no terminal.

The pages live in `app/adapters/htmlauth` and `app/views/sessions` and
`app/views/passwords`; change their markup and wording freely. `auth.Browser`
in the framework makes every security decision: CSRF verification, uniform
failures, throttling (429 with `Retry-After`), the session cookies and
validated return paths.

| Method | Path | Page |
| ------ | ---- | ---- |
| `GET` | `/session/new` | Sign-in form (organization, email, password) |
| `POST` | `/session` | Sign in; 303 to the validated `return_to` or `/` |
| `DELETE` | `/session` | Sign out (`_method=DELETE` form, `sessions/_sign_out` partial) |
| `GET`, `POST` | `/session/mfa` | Authenticator or backup code, when `FORGE_AUTH_MFA_KEY` is set |
| `GET` | `/passwords/new` | Forgot-password form (only when reset emails are configured, see below) |
| `POST` | `/passwords` | Send reset instructions (same answer whether or not the account exists) |
| `GET` | `/passwords/edit?token=...` | Choose a new password from the emailed link |
| `PATCH` | `/passwords` | Change the password; signs every session out |

Guard pages with `middleware.AuthenticateBrowser(htmlauth.SignInPath, page)`
(the wiring `forge generate scaffold` prints does): a signed-out browser is
sent to the sign-in page and back, while JSON clients still get `401`.

### Password-reset emails

The reset pages, and the "Forgot your password?" link, appear only when
outgoing mail is configured; nothing pretends to send otherwise. In
development, write the emails to files (in `.env.local`, or uncomment the
lines in `.env.development`):

```bash
FORGE_MAIL_URL=file:tmp/mail
FORGE_MAIL_FROM="{{.Name}} <no-reply@example.com>"
FORGE_AUTH_PASSWORD_RESET_URL=http://localhost:8080/passwords/edit
```

Restart `forge dev`, request a reset at `/passwords/new` and open the link in
the newest `tmp/mail/*.eml`. In production use your SMTP server (STARTTLS on
587 with `smtp://`, implicit TLS on 465 with `smtps://`) and the public URL:

```bash
FORGE_MAIL_URL=smtps://user:password@smtp.example.com:465
FORGE_MAIL_FROM="{{.Name}} <no-reply@example.com>"
FORGE_AUTH_PASSWORD_RESET_URL=https://app.example.com/passwords/edit
```

`bootstrap.passwordRecoveryDelivery` wires `auth/recoverymail` from these
variables; `FORGE_MAIL_URL` without `FORGE_AUTH_PASSWORD_RESET_URL` stops the
application at startup. See the Forge mail guide (`docs/MAIL.md`).

`db/seeds.go` holds `db.Seed`, which `forge seed` (`{{.Name}} seed`) runs in
one transaction. Keep it idempotent.

## Authentication API (generated with PostgreSQL)

The following endpoints are registered by `bootstrap.Configure`:

| Method | Path | Description |
| ------ | ---- | ----------- |
| `POST` | `/auth/login` | Bearer login; returns an opaque access token unless MFA is required |
| `POST` | `/auth/logout` | Revoke a bearer session |
| `GET` | `/v1/auth/session` | Current authenticated principal |
| `POST` | `/auth/recovery/request` | Request a password reset (non-enumerating response) |
| `POST` | `/auth/recovery/reset` | Reset the password with a recovery token |
| `POST` | `/auth/cookie/login` | Browser login using secure session and CSRF cookies |
| `POST` | `/auth/cookie/logout` | Revoke and clear browser cookies; requires CSRF |

When `FORGE_AUTH_MFA_KEY` is configured, MFA completion routes are also
registered at `/auth/mfa/complete` and `/auth/cookie/mfa/complete`. Browser
cookie routes require a secure same-origin HTTPS `Origin` and unsafe cookie
requests require CSRF validation.

Password recovery emails are sent through `auth/recoverymail` once
`FORGE_MAIL_URL`, `FORGE_MAIL_FROM` and `FORGE_AUTH_PASSWORD_RESET_URL` are set
(see Password-reset emails). Until then the JSON recovery routes stay
fail-closed: they return the same accepted response to avoid account
enumeration, log a generic configuration error and invalidate the token, and
the HTML reset pages are not mounted. No recovery token or recipient is
written to logs.
{{- end}}

| Path | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `cmd/{{.Name}}` | Process entrypoint: `serve` (default), `healthcheck`, `openapi`{{if .DB}}, `migrate`, `rollback`, `seed`{{end}}{{if and .DB .DB.Identity}}, `setup`{{end}} |
| `app/bootstrap` | Composition root: dependencies, readiness checks, routes |
{{- if and .DB .DB.Identity}}
| `app/adapters/htmlauth` | Sign-in, sign-out, two-factor and password-reset pages |
{{- end}}
{{- if .DB}}
| `db/migrations` | SQL migrations, embedded in the binary |
| `db/seeds.go` | Idempotent seed data for `seed` |
{{- end}}
| `Dockerfile` | Production image: static binary, distroless, non-root |
| `compose.yaml` | Local stack running the same image in development mode |
| `compose.observability.yaml`, `observability/` | Opt-in Grafana, Prometheus, Tempo, Loki and OpenTelemetry Collector stack, preconfigured |
| `.env.development` | Development settings for the `forge` commands (no secrets) |
{{- if and .DB .DB.Identity}}
| `.env.local` | Git-ignored local MFA key and private overrides |
{{- end}}

Domain, application, adapter and infrastructure packages live under `app/`
and are added as the application grows.
{{- if and .DB .DB.Identity}}

## Identity

PostgreSQL projects include Forge's identity migrations alongside application
migrations and wire the PostgreSQL identity repository into the generated
composition root.

The generated API exposes:

- `POST /auth/login` for tenant-scoped email/password bearer login;
- `POST /auth/logout` for idempotent bearer-session revocation with security
  audit;
- `GET /v1/auth/session` as an authenticated example returning the current
  subject, tenant, and permissions.

The generated `.env.local` contains a unique development-only key for local
MFA seed encryption. It is git-ignored and loaded by both `forge` and Docker
Compose. Never reuse this development key in production; configure a separate
secret there. If `FORGE_AUTH_MFA_KEY` is configured, the generated app wires
`POST /auth/mfa/complete`. The value must be canonical unpadded base64url for
exactly 32 random bytes and belongs in `.env.local` during development or a
secret manager in deployed environments. It is never stored in the identity
database.

Run `migrate` before serving so the Forge identity tables exist, then
`setup` once to create the first owner. Inviting further users and MFA
enrollment/rotation remain application opt-ins because they require
application policy and step-up authorization. Browser-cookie
routes are available for HTTPS applications. Password recovery delivery is
enabled by the `FORGE_MAIL_*` and `FORGE_AUTH_PASSWORD_RESET_URL` settings
above; any SMTP server works, self-hosted included.
{{- end}}

## API description (OpenAPI)

JSON routes declare their request, response and error types next to the
route (`openapi.Typed` or `openapi.Describe` from
`github.com/rocketinsidecorp/forge-go/openapi`), so the OpenAPI 3.1 document
is generated from the Go types and cannot drift from them. `GET /v1/status`
in `app/bootstrap` is the example; generated resources and `--api`
controllers are described too.

```bash
# Served in development and test (FORGE_OPENAPI_ENABLED=true elsewhere)
curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/openapi.json

# Written from the application without serving it{{if and .DB .DB.Server}} (needs the database, like forge migrate){{end}}
forge openapi --output openapi.json
forge openapi --check openapi.json   # exits 1 when the committed file is stale (CI)
```

In staging and production the document is not served unless
`FORGE_OPENAPI_ENABLED=true`: it lists every documented route. The handler
tests of generated resources check every response against it.

## Observability

Every request gets a trace ID that appears as `trace_id` in its logs, and
HTTP{{if .DB}}, database{{end}} and job metrics are recorded. Nothing leaves the process
until you choose an export path:

```bash
# Prometheus scrape endpoint on a private port (GET /metrics)
FORGE_TELEMETRY_METRICS_ADDR=127.0.0.1:9464 forge dev
# Traces and metrics to an OpenTelemetry Collector (OTLP/HTTP)
FORGE_TELEMETRY_EXPORTER=otlp FORGE_TELEMETRY_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318 forge dev
# Spans in the development log
FORGE_TELEMETRY_EXPORTER=log forge dev
# Every log record, with trace_id and span_id, also to the collector (OTLP/HTTP);
# standard output keeps the same lines. Uses the same FORGE_TELEMETRY_OTLP_* settings.
FORGE_TELEMETRY_LOGS_EXPORTER=otlp FORGE_TELEMETRY_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318 forge dev
```

Go runtime (goroutines, heap, garbage collection){{if .DB}} and database connection pool
(connections in use, idle and the limit, waits){{end}} metrics are always
recorded and reach both `/metrics` and OTLP.

Set `FORGE_TELEMETRY_SERVICE_NAME={{.Name}}` in deployments. Incoming
`traceparent` headers are ignored unless `FORGE_TELEMETRY_TRUST_TRACEPARENT=true`
(only behind a proxy that sets or sanitizes them). A metrics address other
than loopback, such as `0.0.0.0:9464` in a container, needs
`FORGE_TELEMETRY_METRICS_PUBLIC=true`; never publish that port. See the Forge
observability guide (docs/OBSERVABILITY.md) for every variable.

### Observability stack: Grafana, Prometheus, Tempo and Loki

`compose.observability.yaml` adds a preconfigured stack to the compose
project: an OpenTelemetry Collector, Prometheus (metrics), Tempo (traces),
Loki (logs) and Grafana with the data sources and a starter dashboard already
provisioned. It is opt-in: `docker compose up` does not start it. Everything
stays on your machine and every published port is bound to `127.0.0.1`.

```bash
# 1. Start it (Grafana refuses to start without an admin password you choose)
export GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD="$(openssl rand -base64 18)"   # or put it in .env
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.observability.yaml up --build

# 2. Generate some data
curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/status

# 3. Open Grafana: http://127.0.0.1:3000, user admin, the password above
#    Dashboards > "Forge application"  request rate, latency, errors, database, connection pool, Go runtime, jobs, logs, traces
#    Explore > Tempo                   traces; "Logs for this span" opens the matching log lines
#    Explore > Loki                    logs; a trace_id links to its trace
#    Explore > Prometheus              metrics, also at http://127.0.0.1:9090

# 4. Stop it (keeps the data); add -v to delete traces, logs and metrics too
# compose interpolates the whole file, so GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD must be set for down too
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.observability.yaml down
```

The app sends traces, metrics and logs to the collector over OTLP
(`FORGE_TELEMETRY_LOGS_EXPORTER=otlp` is set by the overlay), which forwards them
to Tempo, Prometheus and Loki. To use another host port set `GRAFANA_PORT` or
`PROMETHEUS_PORT`. The app logs only what it or the framework logs (startup,
errors, your `logger.InfoContext(ctx, ...)` calls); each record made with a traced
context carries `trace_id` and `span_id`, which is what ties logs and traces
together. Output that does not go through the logger (a crash's stack trace, for
example) is only in `docker compose logs`. The
stack keeps seven days of data and is meant for development, not production.
To export from an app started with `forge dev`, run
`FORGE_TELEMETRY_SERVICE_NAME={{.Name}} FORGE_TELEMETRY_EXPORTER=otlp FORGE_TELEMETRY_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:4318 forge dev`
while the stack runs, adding `FORGE_TELEMETRY_LOGS_EXPORTER=otlp` to send logs too.

## Credentials

Secrets that do not belong in `.env` files live encrypted in
`config/credentials/<environment>.json.enc`, which is committed. The key
stays out of git: `config/credentials/development.key` (created by `forge new`,
mode 0600) or the `FORGE_MASTER_KEY` variable in deployed environments.

```bash
forge credentials edit                          # edit development credentials in $EDITOR
forge credentials show                          # structure only; values are redacted
forge credentials init --environment production # new key and encrypted file for production
FORGE_MASTER_KEY="$(cat config/credentials/production.key)"  # what the deployed app needs
```

Back up every key; without it the encrypted file cannot be read. Read values
with `credentials.Open` from `github.com/rocketinsidecorp/forge-go/credentials`.

## Deploy

The image runs `serve` by default and reads `FORGE_*` variables. Production
refuses insecure settings: TLS (or `FORGE_HTTP_TRANSPORT=trusted-proxy` behind
a TLS-terminating proxy){{if and .DB .DB.ProductionTLS}} and {{.DB.ProductionTLS}} on
`FORGE_DATABASE_URL`{{end}}.

Behind a reverse proxy the application sees the proxy's address unless it
trusts that proxy's `X-Forwarded-For`. List the proxy networks so sign-in
throttles, rate limits and audit records count each client
(`forge deploy` sets this to the forge-proxy network automatically; empty
trusts nobody):

```bash
docker run --rm -e FORGE_HTTP_TRUSTED_PROXIES=172.18.0.0/16 {{.Name}}
```
{{- if and .DB (not .DB.Server)}}

The image stores the SQLite database in `/app/storage/production.db`; mount a
persistent volume there and run a single application instance.
{{- end}}
{{- if .DB}}

Run migrations before rolling out a release with the same image:

```bash
docker run --rm -e FORGE_ENV=production -e FORGE_DATABASE_URL=... {{.Name}} migrate
```

Concurrent `migrate` runs are serialized by {{.DB.MigrationLock}}.
{{- end}}
